Dagric OS — why 10-dagric-privacy.json exists, and why the reasoning is here
and not inside it.

Chromium reads every *.json in this directory and merges them. It also lists
every key it does not recognise in chrome://policy as "Unknown policy", in red.
A "_comment" array would therefore turn the one page a careful buyer opens to
CHECK our claims into a page with a red row on it. The policy file stays pure
JSON; the explanation lives in this file, which Chromium ignores.

THE GAP THIS CLOSED. Before this, there was no Chromium policy anywhere in the
Dagric tree, on an image that ships Chromium. site/privacy.html describes how
this machine resolves names, and that page's own comment block records that a
claim was once made about one browser without matching the other — and calls it
"the version of this mistake that does the most damage, because the reader who
checks is the procurement officer". Chromium's policy must therefore stand on
its own documented behavior rather than borrowing a Firefox claim.

DnsOverHttpsMode "automatic", not "secure".
  automatic  upgrades to DNS-over-HTTPS when the resolver the network handed us
             is one with a known DoH endpoint, and stays on plaintext when it is
             not.
  secure     refuses to resolve at all without DoH. That breaks school and
             corporate split-horizon DNS, and it breaks every captive portal,
             because a portal works by hijacking DNS until you sign in. A
             browser that cannot resolve anything is not a privacy win.
This is Chromium's explicit Dagric policy; Firefox remains on Debian/Mozilla's
own configuration.

No DnsOverHttpsTemplates on purpose. Pinning a template would route every name
this machine looks up through one company that we picked, on a product sold on
not making that kind of choice for the owner. "automatic" keeps the resolver the
owner's own network chose and encrypts the transport to it.

PasswordLeakDetectionEnabled is deliberately NOT set. Turning it off would look
like a privacy win and is a security loss: the check is k-anonymous — a hashed
prefix, not the password — and it warns people whose credentials are already in
a breach corpus. Dagric does not trade a real protection for the appearance of
one.

There is intentionally no Firefox equivalent. Dagric redistributes Debian's
Firefox ESR without a Dagric distribution policy so the browser's defaults and
first-run behavior remain unmodified.
